#edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186416852 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the following relation defined on the set $A=\\{△,♢,♡\\}.$\n\n$$\nR=\\{(△,♡),(△,♢),(♢,♡)\\}\n$$\n\nSelect the options that make the following reasoning correct:\n\nWe have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(△,△){{field-1}}R,\\,(♢,♢){{field-2}}R,\\,(♡,♡){{field-3}}R\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since\n{{field-4}}{{field-5}},\n\nthe relation is\n{{field-6}}." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186416852 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "02d6ada8-eb50-5039-9152-d73d78002d54" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186416852 #edb/ref 1074 "A binary relation $R,$ defined on a set $A,$ is \n\n- *reflexive* if $(x,x)\\in R$ for all $x\\in A,$\n\n- *irreflexive* if $(x,x)\\notin R$ for all $x\\in A.$\n\nIf some pairs of the form $(x,x)$ belong to a relation and others do not, then the relation is neither reflexive nor irreflexive.\n\nIn our case, the relation is defined on $\\{△,♢,♡\\},$ and we have\n\n$$\n(△,△)\\notin R,\\,(♢,♢)\\notin R,\\,(♡,♡)\\notin R.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, we can conclude the following:\n\n$\\,$ Since $(x,x)\\notin R$ $\\text{for all }x\\in A,$ the relation is $\\text{irreflexive}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186416853 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186416844 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186416853 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186416845 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186416853 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186416846 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186416853 #edb/ref 1069 "q-213244" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186416853 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186416853 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider a relation $R$ that is defined on the set $A=\\{\\alpha ,\\beta ,\\gamma \\}.$ Given that $R$ is reflexive, enter the missing values in the adjacency matrix.\n\n|  | $\\alpha $ | $\\beta $ | $\\gamma $ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\alpha $ | {{field-1}} | $1$ | $1$ |\n| $\\beta $ | $0$ | {{field-2}} | $0$ |\n| $\\gamma $ | $0$ | $0$ | {{field-3}} |" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186416853 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "08d898ff-f576-5e6a-bdf8-9d2e027a5e42" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186416853 #edb/ref 1074 "A binary relation $R,$ defined on a set $A,$ is \n\n- *reflexive* if $(x,x)\\in R$ for all $x\\in A,$\n\n- *irreflexive* if $(x,x)\\notin R$ for all $x\\in A.$\n\nIf some pairs of the form $(x,x)$ belong to a relation and others do not, then the relation is neither reflexive nor irreflexive.\n\nSince $R$ must be reflexive, we require \n\n$$\n(\\alpha ,\\alpha )\\in R,\\,(\\beta ,\\beta )\\in R,\\,(\\gamma ,\\gamma )\\in R.\n$$\n\nThis means that the diagonal elements of the adjacency matrix must all be $1,$  as shown below:\n\n|  | $\\alpha $ | $\\beta $ | $\\gamma $ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\alpha $ | $1$ | $1$ | $1$ |\n| $\\beta $ | $0$ | $1$ | $0$ |\n| $\\gamma $ | $0$ | $0$ | $1$ |" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186416854 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186416826 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186416854 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186416840 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186416854 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186416850 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186416854 #edb/ref 1069 "q-212140" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186416854 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186416854 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the following relation defined on the set $A=\\{0,1,2\\}.$ \n\n$$\nR=\\{(0,0),(0,1),(1,1),(2,0),(2,1)\\}\n$$\n\nSelect the options that make the following reasoning correct:\n\nSince \n{{field-1}}{{field-2}}, \n\nthe relation is \n{{field-3}}." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186416854 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "2bcd445f-52b3-5de6-8b46-eb7aa3382e40" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186416854 #edb/ref 1074 "A binary relation $R,$ defined on a set $A,$ is \n\n- *reflexive* if $(x,x)\\in R$ for all $x\\in A,$\n\n- *irreflexive* if $(x,x)\\notin R$ for all $x\\in A.$\n\nIf some pairs of the form $(x,x)$ belong to a relation and others do not, then the relation is neither reflexive nor irreflexive.\n\nIn our case, the relation is defined on $\\{0,1,2\\},$ and we have\n\n$$\n(0,0)\\in R,\\,(1,1)\\in R,\\,(2,2)\\notin R.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can conclude the following:\n\n$\\,$ Since $(2,2)\\notin R$ $\\text{and }(1,1)\\in R,$ the relation is $\\text{neither reflexive nor irreflexive}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
